{{also|Stiver}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|nl|stuiver}}, cognate with {{cog|gml|-}} ''[[stüver]]''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈstaɪvə/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|historical|money}} A small [[Dutch]] coin worth one twentieth of a [[guilder]].
# Anything of small [[value]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1761|author=w:Laurence Sterne|title=The Life & Opinions of Tristram Shandy, Gentleman|volume=4|page=223|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2003
|passage=’Tis not worth a single '''stiver''', said the bandy-leg'd drummer.}}
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|[[s:Moby-Dick (1851) US edition/Chapter 16|chapter 16]]|text=[A]ll hands, including the captain, received certain shares of the profits called lays {{...}} And though the 275th lay was what they call a rather long lay, yet it was better than nothing; and if we had a lucky voyage, might pretty nearly pay for the clothing I would wear out on it, not to speak of my three years' beef and board, for which I would not have to pay one '''stiver'''.}}

